Week 4 Review — RAG Evaluation, Observability & Caching (Q&A Style)

A compiled revision document from the Week 4 Q&A session: evaluating RAG, tracing and monitoring it, caching it, and debugging it when it gets worse.

Topic 1: RAG Evaluation — Retrieval Metrics
The week's pipeline: RAG → Evaluation → Tracing → Monitoring → Optimization. Many candidates stop at "RAG → answer". The skill is knowing when it is wrong, where, and why.
Two halves, two kinds of failure. A RAG system has a retrieval stage (did we find the right chunks?) and a generation stage (given those chunks, did the LLM write a good, faithful answer?). Checking only the final answer can't tell you which stage broke, so evaluation has separate metrics for each.
Example: the user asks about the refund window and the system says 14 days, but the policy says 30. Possible causes:
• Retrieval: a stale old chunk saying 14 days was retrieved, the right chunk ranked too low or was missed, or bad chunking separated "30 days" from the word "refund".
• Generation: the right chunk was retrieved, but the model hallucinated or ignored the context.
Context precision. Of the chunks retrieved, how many were actually relevant? If 2 of 5 retrieved chunks are useful, precision is 2/5 = 40%. Low precision means junk in the context.
Context recall. Of all the information needed to answer, how much did we actually retrieve? If the answer needs three facts and the top 5 chunks hold only two, recall is low. Low recall means something important never reached the LLM.
Retrieval relevance. A more general score of how well the retrieved chunks match the question, often judged by an LLM. Precision and recall are more specific about which chunks were useful.
Memory aid (librarian): precision asks whether the pile she handed you is clean. Recall asks whether she found everything you needed.
The trade-off. Retrieving 20 chunks instead of 5 casts a wider net: recall goes up (more likely to catch the needed fact), but precision goes down (more junk). A bigger net catches more fish, and more old boots.
The reranker resolves it. First-stage retrieval casts the wide net (high recall), then the cross-encoder reranks and you keep only the best few (precision back up). The reranker only reorders what it is given, so it cannot fix low recall. If the needed chunk never made the shortlist, nothing downstream can conjure it.
Debugging rule of thumb:
• Low recall: fix first-stage retrieval. Retrieve more candidates, add hybrid search, rewrite the query, or fix the chunking.
• Low precision: fix the reranker or reduce the number of chunks you keep.

Topic 2: Generation and Operational Metrics
Faithfulness. Is every claim in the answer supported by the retrieved chunks? If the model adds something the chunks never said, faithfulness drops. In RAG, this failure is essentially what hallucination means: the model makes something up, or ignores the context and leans on its own training knowledge.
Answer relevance. Does the answer actually address the question the user asked? Faithfulness and relevance are independent:
• User asks "What's the refund window?" and the model gives an accurate, fully supported paragraph about the cancellation process. Faithfulness is high, answer relevance is low (grounded but off-topic).
• You need both. One catches made-up answers, the other catches off-topic ones.
Hallucination. The failure itself: claims not grounded in the retrieved context. Faithfulness is the metric that measures it.
Reading the metrics together (diagnosis table).
What moved
Stage to suspect
Recall down
First-stage retrieval, chunking, embeddings, query rewriting
Precision down
Reranker, number of chunks kept, junk in the database
Faithfulness down (retrieval metrics fine)
Generation: prompt, model version
Answer relevance down
Generation: prompt drifting from the question
Operational metrics. Latency, token usage, cost, error rate.
Cost follows tokens. If cost doubles overnight with no code change, look for anything that inflates tokens sent to the LLM:
• Ingestion produced bigger or messier chunks, so each prompt carries more text.
• More chunks being retrieved per query.
• Users sending longer questions.
• A retry loop silently repeating calls after errors (which is why error rate sits next to cost).
• The provider changing pricing or the model version.
Track tokens per request and cost per request over time to see exactly when the jump happened.
Latency needs per-stage timing. A single total can't say whether rewriting, retrieval, reranking, or the LLM call is slow. Tracing gives each step its own timed span.
Averages hide the worst cases. If 95 requests take 1 second and 5 take 30, the average looks fine while some users suffer. Use percentiles:
• p95 = 20 seconds means 95% of requests finished in 20 seconds or less, so the slowest 5% took longer.
• A 2-second average with a 20-second p95 means most users are happy but roughly 1 in 20 waits 20 seconds or more. That is a real problem the average hides, and those users may give up or retry, adding load.
• Watch p95 and p99, not just the mean.

