The workflow, with one running example. You changed chunk size from 500 to 800 and want to know if the system improved.
1. Dataset: write about 50 realistic questions with ideal answers ("What's the refund window?" with "30 days"). Store them in LangSmith.
2. Experiment: LangSmith runs every question through your RAG system, recording a trace per question. The batch of results is the experiment.
3. Scoring: evaluators grade each answer. Some are plain code (does "30" appear in the answer?). Others are LLM-as-judge, scoring things like faithfulness.
4. Comparing: repeat with the old chunk size and view both experiments side by side. For example, faithfulness 0.82 to 0.88, but latency rose. Now you have evidence.
Why not just try a few questions by hand? A handful of hand-picked questions covers few scenarios and can mislead. A fixed dataset gives the same questions and same scoring each time, so the only variable is your change.
Traces vs experiments (common mix-up). A trace explains what happened inside one request, so it is for debugging. An experiment compares versions across the whole dataset. A user complains about one bad answer: open the trace. You want to know if a new prompt is better overall: run an experiment over the dataset (the dataset alone does not answer it).
Finding what got worse. If faithfulness drops from 0.88 to 0.70:
1. Open the experiment and compare the two runs row by row, or sort by faithfulness, so the questions that dropped most rise to the top.
2. Open the trace for one of them. Did retrieval bring back the right chunks? If yes, generation is at fault (prompt, model). If the right chunk is missing, look upstream at chunking and retrieval.
LLM-as-judge. A second model gets the question, the retrieved chunks and the answer, and scores it. For faithfulness it typically splits the answer into individual claims and checks each against the chunks. Ragas and DeepEval are libraries that give you these judges ready-made, so you don't write the judging prompts yourself. They work on records of question, retrieved contexts and answer, from a dataset, a test script, or exported trace data. They do not need traces.
Can you trust a score of 0.95? Check two things: is the dataset big and realistic enough (ten questions can be luck, and questions should resemble real user questions), and does the judge match human judgement? The judge is an LLM, so it can be wrong or biased. Spot-check by reading a sample of answers yourself and seeing whether you agree with the scores.
LangSmith: open source or not? Not open source. It is proprietary SaaS, and self-hosting is only on the Enterprise plan. But the hosted Developer tier is free and includes about 5,000 traces per month, which is plenty for learning and for the Week 4 project. You create an account, set an API key as an environment variable, and add a tracing hook to your code. Every request then appears as a trace in the web dashboard, where you upload datasets, run experiments and attach evaluators. It works with plain Python and FastAPI, though it fits best with LangChain or LangGraph. (Pricing changes often, so check the current page.)
Alternatives. Langfuse and Arize Phoenix are open source and free to self-host, covering similar ground. Galileo is a comparable commercial platform, with comparisons often citing it as strong on RAG evaluation and groundedness. Several of those comparisons come from vendors, so treat them as opinions. The concepts transfer directly between tools.
Prompt testing. Same workflow: run the old and new prompt over the same dataset, score both with the same evaluators, and compare the experiments before shipping the change.

Topic 4: Observability
Three signals.
• Logs: individual events as lines of text, with no built-in link between lines. With 50 requests running at once, log lines interleave and you can't tell which belongs to which request.
• Metrics: numbers aggregated over time, like p95 latency, error rate, tokens per request, cost. They tell you that something changed, and when.
• Traces: one request's journey. A trace ties everything for that request together under a single trace ID, as a tree of timed steps called spans.
What a RAG trace looks like. Request → FastAPI → RAG, which contains Query transformation, Retrieval, Reranking, and the LLM call → Response. You can see where the time went and which step failed. A slow request becomes easy to diagnose: open its trace and see the reranker took 6 of the 8 seconds.
What to track per request: latency (per stage), tokens, cost, retrieval quality, errors, and the LLM response.
The debugging rhythm. Metrics tell you something is wrong and when. Traces show where. Logs show the details.
• Error rate jumped from 1% to 10% at 3 pm: spot it on the metrics dashboard, then open traces from around 3 pm filtered for failures to see which step broke (say the LLM call timing out), then read the logs for the exact error message.
• p95 latency doubled this morning: check the metric to confirm when it started, then open slow traces and look at the spans to see which step grew, then ask what changed around that time.
OpenTelemetry (OTel). An open standard for producing traces, metrics and logs. Think USB-C: without it, every tool (LangSmith, Datadog, Grafana, Galileo) has its own plug, and switching tools means rewriting your instrumentation. With OTel you instrument the FastAPI app once, and the data can flow to any backend. Change tools later and you change where the data is sent, not your code. It is the same idea as MCP: a shared protocol so you don't build a custom connection for every pair.
Distributed tracing. One request crosses several services: FastAPI, the vector database, the LLM provider. A trace ID travels along with the request like a baton, so every service adds its spans to the same trace, and they join into one trace instead of separate fragments.
